{"text": "The Olfactory Nerve Contains Two\nPopulations of Glia, Identified Both In Vivo\nand In Vitro\n\nSARAH K. PIXLEY\nDepartment of Anatomy and Cell Biolog. Univer of Cincinnati Collegeof Medicine,\nGineinnets Ohio $5209 O68\n\nKEY WORDS Ensheathing cell:\n\n\u2018A2B5, Enteric glia\n\nABSTRACT\n\nSchwann cells, Astrocytes, GFAP, $-100, 217C,\n\n\u2018The peripheral olfactory nervous system exhibits, uniquely, neuronal\n\ncell body replacement and reestablishment of central connections in adult maminals, The\nrole ofthe olfactory nerve glia in these phenomena is unknown, but information might be\nprovided by in vitro systems. This paper reports on the characterization of olfactory nerve\nBlia in dissociated cell cultures of newborn rat nasal mucosal tissues. The predominant\ntype of glial cell resembled Schwann cells and immunostained for the $1100 protein,\n\nfound in all glial cell types; gli\n\nfibrillary acidie protein (GFAP), found in astrocytes and\n\n\u2018nonmyelinating Schwann cells; and showed binding of 217C, a monoclonal Schwann-cell\nmarker that binds to the low-affinity NGF receptor in glioma cells. They were negative\nfor A2B5. The Schwann-cell-like olfactory glia changed morphology upon culturing in\nserum-free\u2019 medium, with further shape changes after plating on laminin. Plating on\nlaminin increased cell numbers. A second population, found only after GFAP-immuno,\nstaining, was astrocyte-iike in morphology and represented approximately 10 percent of\nall glial cells. These were S-100., A2B5-. and 217C-negative, a unique glial cell immune\nlogical profile. At low dilutions of anti-GPAP (1/10,000), or with weak fluorescent second:\n\nary antibodies. astrocyte-like glia were immunostained but Schwann-cell\n\nke glia were\n\nnot detectable. Astrocyte-like glia were not an artifact of the dissection, since they were\ndetectabie in tissue sections of newborn-rat olfactory nerves immunostained with a low\ndilution of anti-GFAP. The presence of two types of glial cells in culture suggests similar,\n\nities between olfactory glia and enteric gla,\n\n\u00a9 1992 Wiley-Lie, Ine\n\nINTRODUCTION\n\nOlfactory nerve glial cells (ONGCs) may play impor-\ntant roles in support of regenerative events occurring. in\nthe peripheral olfactory system, The cell bodies of the\nprimary olfactory sensory elements, the olfactory recep.\ntor neurons (ORNs), are in the olfactory epithelium lin.\ning of the nasal eavity. ORNs are continually replaced\nfrom an epithelial supply of stem cells throughout the\nadult life of mammals (Farbman, 1990; Graziadei and\n\u2018Monti-Graziadei, 1978). Low numbers of ORNs are gen:\nerated continually in the adult (Graziadei and Monti\nGraziadei, 1978), while extensive neurogenesis. and\nneuronal replacement is scen after damage to the epi\n\n61992 Wiley-Liss, te.\n\nthelium, bulb, or nerve, which causes large-scale ORN\ndegeneration (Costanzo and Graziadei, 1983; Hurtt\nct al, 1988). Newly differentiated ORNs extend axons\nthat are ensheathed by glial celis as the axons exit the\nolfactory epithelium. Ensheathed axons plus connec.\ntive tissue elements make up the olfactory nerve bun-\ndles that penetrate the cribriform plate, enter the cra\nnial cavity, and form the nervesfiber layer of the\n\nReine Mare 271991; ape September 101881\n\nTLLb6S48",
